<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">United States Virgin Islands</h1><p class="st-meta">1917 CE – present · capital Charlotte Amalie</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The former Danish West Indies, bought by the United States in 1917 to guard the Panama Canal approaches and now an unincorporated US territory.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Fearing that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> might acquire a base in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a> bought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-thomas-u-s-virgin-islands">Saint Thomas</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-john-u-s-virgin-islands">Saint John</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-croix">Saint Croix</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> for 25 million dollars in gold. The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/danish-west-indies">Danish West Indies</a> were handed over on 31 March 1917, now celebrated as Transfer Day.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Until 1931 the islands were run by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-navy">US Navy</a>, with naval officers as governors, and the inhabitants became US citizens only in 1927. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/organic-acts-of-the-virgin-islands">Organic Acts</a> of 1936 and 1954 created a local legislature, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melvin-evans">Melvin Evans</a> became the first elected governor in 1970. The decline of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> and the end of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prohibition-in-the-united-states">Prohibition</a> hurt the economy, which later turned to tourism, cruise shipping and, on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-croix">Saint Croix</a>, a large oil refinery that closed in 2012.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>The islands remain an unincorporated, organised territory with a non-voting delegate in Congress. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hurricane-irma">Hurricanes Irma</a> and Maria struck in 2017, and the territory continues to debate its status and its Danish and African heritage.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/united-states-virgin-islands.jpg" alt="United States Virgin Islands" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Percival Wilson Sparks. sparksfamilyassn.org. Retrieved on 2026-05-26. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_United_States_Virgin_Islands.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1917 CE – present</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Charlotte Amalie</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Melvin Evans</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Protestantism and Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English and Virgin Islands Creole</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>United States Virgin Islands</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>US Virgin Islands, Virgin Islands of the United States</dd></dl></aside></div>
